   As I said, they came to the conclusion that the only option on these
   was the statist one -- since the colonial governments weren't doing well,
   maybe the federal one could. They had no other vision of how things could
   be done so they resorted to the only paradigm they had. This is
   consistant with my argument.

No, what I mean by "need" is a perceived problem that people want
solved.  There *were* needs; the method they chose to solve them may
be one that you would not prefer, but the needs were real, and
involved geographic areas larger than the individual states.

But beside all that, the crux of the question is:  Are there "needs"
in society that "require" some sort of organization on scales larger
than about 1 mile?  (I'm not worrying whether the organization is a
"government", a "corporation", a "cooperative", or whatever -- in the
end, whatever organization-form is available will be pressed into
service if there is a perceived need.)

The ultra-anarchist answer is "no", all such "needs" are fictions.
Statists, libertarians, communists, etc. will generally say "yes", but
propose different systems for satisfying the need.

My impression is that once commerce commonly reaches a given distance,
there is a great "need" for organization on that scale, to standardize
the commercial law over the trading area.  (Probably to reduce the
transaction cost of negotiating commercial law.)  This seems to happen
even in cases where there seems to be no coercive power to force
standardization -- witness the adoption of the Uniform Commercial
Code by the states in the US.

Dale

