Date: Sat, 13 Feb 93 22:48:15 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

Thanks to Robin for his patience in staying with this discussion.
I have been looking at some more econ books and getting a better
feel for CAPM theory and how it might apply to commodity and Idea
Futures.

The CAPM theory is interesting because it seems to provide two rather
contradictory bits of advice for how to set up a portfolio.  On the one
hand, the theory suggests that maximum diversification is ideal.  In
theory, you would own some of every stock, bond, option, etc. in the world.
This portfolio would have maximum diversity.  Then, you judge whether
the risk level of this portfolio is right for you.  If it's too risky,
put only a fraction of your money into this, and put the rest into a
riskless investment.  If it's not risky enough, buy the portfolio using
margin to get more leverage and increase risk and return.  That's all
you have to do.

On the other hand, CAPM also provides a way of analyzing portfolios
in terms of "beta", which is a measure of how well price movements in
the portfolio correlate with movements in the ideal "whole market"
portfolio described above.  The return of a portfolio should depend on
its beta.  So presumably an alternate way to set up a portfolio is to
choose the beta level and get the corresponding risk and return.

So, we seem to have two different approaches for setting up an ideal
portfolio of a desired beta; one has you own a little bit of everything,
while the other might have you own only assets of the desired beta.

With respect to Robin's "contingent assets", the first approach would
if followed literally have you own both sides of the bet, which is the
same as owning the underlying asset.  This is why I argued that CAPM
theory would not lead to investments in these markets.

However, Robin is right that such maximal diversification is really not
necessary in practice.  Instead of owning all sides of every bet, you
could buy a few dozen or hundred bets, selected so that their various
risks balanced out.  This might involve taking rather contradictory
positions - betting that human lifespan might be increased to 200
years, while betting that no cure for cancer will ever be found, for
example.  But you could set up a portfolio which included contingent
assets without paying any penalty, so Robin is right about this.

This does raise the question of whether there is any value to adding a
randomly chosen basket of contingent assets to an otherwise
thoroughly-diversified portfolio in terms of diversification.  (Put
another way, would the maximally-diversified portfolio described above
include Idea Futures?)  It seems to me that there can be no benefit to
this, that you can always do at least as well simply by buying the
underlying securities, but my guesses have been wrong before so I am
willing to consider this.

Of course, not all investors are happy just to set up a diverse portfolio
and take their return.  I suspect that an Idea Futures market would
mostly include people with very different reasons for their investments
than this.  I would think most bettors would enter positions with
positive correlations, intentionally taking on more risk because they are
convinced that they are smarter than the market as a whole on certain
issues.

I also can make an argument, now that I have learned a little more about
how these markets work, for commodity futures being zero-sum, even though
Robin's Idea Futures are not.  This is how it goes.  All commodity
contracts are created in pairs, with a long and a short position.  The
change in value of one position is equal to the negative of the change in
value of the other position.  Hence the sum of the changes in value is
zero.

This is not true for one of Robin's contingent assets where the
underlying security is changing in value.  (It is true if the underlying
security has constant value.)  For Robin's contingent assets, it is
possible for the change in value of one side of the bet not to be the
negative of the change in value of the other side (because the underlying
security has changed price).  Therefore Idea Futures, which are based on
this concept of contingent assets, are not zero sum.

I don't think commodity futures can be expressed in terms of contingent
assets.

Hal


