Date: Sat, 13 Feb 93 23:10:56 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: BOOK: Fire's ending disappointing?

SPOILER WARNING!  SPOILER WARNING!  THIS POST DISCUSSES THE ENDING OF
"A FIRE UPON THE DEEP".

Don't read on unless you already know how this book ends, or don't care
if you find out here!

Although I enjoyed Vinge's book, especially the parts that took place out
in space, the ending disappointed me.  For one thing, it was a little
unclear to me what actually happened.  Here is my guess:

The countermeasure caused the Zones in some large part of the galaxy to
move outwards, so that most of this part of the galaxy is now in the Slow
Zone.  This killed the Straumli Perversion along with any other gods
still alive since they can't survive in the Slow Zone.  It probably also
destroyed most of the civilizations in the Beyond since their technology
no longer works.

The Zones had been slowly shrinking throughout recorded history.  What
had they been shrinking from?  Presumably an earlier incident just like
this, when the Perversion had its previous try at taking over the galaxy.
It had been defeated then as it has now, and by the same method.  Only as
hundreds of millions of years have gone by has the gradual shrinkage of
the Zones brought more civilizations into the Beyond and allowed high
technology to become widespread.  Now, this cycle begins again, and many
millions of years will have to pass before the state of the Zones is
restored to what it was at the beginning of the book.

Now, I may be filling in too many blanks to come up with this
explanation, but this is what Vinge seemed to me to be saying.  And my
problem with this is that the ending of the book involves a catastrophe
of unimaginable magnitude!  In fact, if it weren't for the fact that it
was apparently necessary to defeat the Blight, such an event would
normally be considered horribly evil.  You've killed God knows how many
people, not to mention considerable super-intelligences; you've thrown
perhaps billions of civilizations into the Dark Ages for hundreds of
millions of years.  Pardon me if I don't feel like cheering.  I felt very
disappointed.  Vinge himself has a character say, "In some ways the
Revenge was a worse thing than the Blight itself."  I don't know, maybe
he didn't like the ending, either. :-)

I also thought that the ending was weak dramatically.  I'd like to see
some suffering on the part of the Blight.  Maybe some realization in its
vast intelligence, as it sees that massive tidal wave of doom coming
toward its, feels its connections deeper in the galaxy ripped away, that
it had been defeated.  Remember how poignant it was in 2001 when Hal was
stripped down to sub-human intelligence?  (That scene still makes me mad! :-)
Here we have a real evil, and we don't get to see it die.

The same thing was true, on a lesser scale, with the planetary action.
We never get to see Steel, a really disgusting character, get his just
deserts.  He even gets the last word in his duel with Flenser before the
author cuts away.

As I said, I did enjoy many parts of the book.  But after seeing what all
the characters went through, I wish the ending could have made their
travails a little more worthwhile.

Hal

