Date: Sun, 14 Feb 93 00:27:02 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECON: Predicting the Stock Market

>Myself, I can never tell whether a price increase will continue to go
>up, level off and stay high, or turn around and come down.  Is this
>a lack of training, or just an efficient market in action?
>
>Hal

Nobody can consistently forecast the market. Or at least there have been no
publicly disclosed, convincing demonstrations. (That some folks, such as
Peter Lynch and Warren Buffett seem to do very well seems to contradict
this. More on this later.)

"Technicians" may claim otherwise, but there seem to be no simple
principles, no parallels to "momentum." Thus, an "upward trend" is as
likely to turn down as to continue up--much like noise.

There are all kinds of caveats, which if I don't mention, others will:

* this is not to say the market (e.g., the price histories of stocks) _is_
just noise, as obvious non-noisy changes occur. But to an investor lacking
specific knowledge ("side information" in information theory terms, or
"insider information" to others), the short term behavior (and maybe the
long term behavior as well) is noise-like.

* a fairly new theory describes markets as being in a state of
"self-organized criticality," like a pile of sand with more sand being
dropped on it: the sand pile occasionally rearranges itself (analogous to
sudden market corrections, upward or downward) but basically remains "far
from equilibrium" as the new sand falls. (Apparently this has given former
East Bloc economists some comfort that the booms and busts of capitalism
are grounded in theory and are not "defects" of capitalism.)

* that simple strategies for beating the market have not been found (or
reported, as I noted before) may mean the overall market is
"incompressible," in the sense of Kolmogorov-Chaitin complexity (which I
wrote about around early December).

* there are some money managers and investors who have done very well in
the market, just as there are others who have done very poorly. Certainly
some folks are fools (I think it was Perry who noted that many commodities
investors are ignorant), which means that merely betting _against_ them may
be a winning strategy. (Analogously to betting on lottery numbers that are
_not_ favored by the masses...if many, many lottery ticket buyers are
betting on  relatively few numbers, then buying the other numbers may be a
better deal.) Also, "fundamentals" play a role...investors like Peter Lynch
and Warren Buffett carefully analyze their companies.

There's a lot more to say.

By the way, many of these topics, related to stock markets, gambling,
complexity theory, compressibility, inference, hypothesis testing, etc.,
are covered in a wonderful book called "Elements of Information Theory," by
Cover and Thomas, 1991. There's even a section on how Kolmogorov-Chaitin
algorithmic complexity explains why Occam's Razor works! There's also an
information-theoretic treatment of asset portfolio pricing, with some of
the same arguments about asset allocation as a function of risk that Hal
Finney referred to earlier tonight. (But I haven't read the section
yet...arguing about zero-sum games seems to be a zero-sum game these last
few weeks.)

-Tim May


