Date: Sun, 14 Feb 93 17:13:00 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: NNET / BRAIN: The Case of the Photocopied Holograms

Instances of replicating information patterns that contain some popular,
probably humorous text, are quite common.  An example from a while back was
the 'Guide To Safe Fax' that was emailed and faxed across offices worldwide. 

I've recently come across an interesting variation on the theme, at loose here
in the UK.  It's photocopied sheets of paper, which at first sight appear to
contain random squiggles and dots.  Yet they are said to be 'holograms' -- 3D
images can be perceived after staring for typically about one minute.  And
they work, too!  I wonder if anyone else has seen these, or knows where they
originated.

This is how they appear in normal vision:-  Looking at each individual
component, the most complex squiggle is half an inch to a few inches in size,
consisting of maybe dozens of branches.  Smaller individuals include one
that appears like a distorted Pi, an 'L', an assymetrical 'T' with little
lines hanging off the bottom and right corner, etc, going right down to mere
specks.  Viewing the sheet in the landscape position, features are repeated
horizontally at regular intervals of about 1.1 inches.  But the whole thing
looks just like noise -- no rhyme or reason to it.

But let's get back to the 3D images and the correct way to perceive them.  I
originally thought that you had to focus on a particular spot, and the result
was somehow related to the well-known effect of staring at the same spot for
over about 50 seconds.  (The whole image starts to dim -- change the image on
the retina slightly and it reverts to normal -- refocus on the original spot
and the condition reappears instantly.)  With this approach no image appears.
You have to hold the paper about twelve inches from your eyes and look at it
fairly centrally *without* focusing on it.  The eyes must be focused as on a
distant object.  And it is not necessary to keep 'looking' at the same spot.
But the paper must be viewed in the landscape position.  After a little 
practice, the 3D image appears well within a minute.

The images are not totally 3D -- there are about 20 different 'layers', 
themselves flat, but each apparently at a different depth.  Tilt the paper
vertically or horizontally and the image remains, but there is no inbuilt
parallax.  Rotate more than about ten degrees from the landscape orientation
and the image vanishes.

One sheet provides an image of six spheres, arranged in a circle to look like
a lumpy donut.  The 'tops' of each sphere are the 'highest' points on the
image, and the centre of the central crater has a depth of about 20 layers.
Around the other side of the 'donut' is a shallow 'moat', less than half the
depth of the crater.  And the border consists of a flat region, a few levels
below the peaks of the spheres, and a few levels above the moat.  But the
whole image is very sharp.

In another example, most of the central area is down in a dip, but contains
'high' areas which clearly spell out letters.  The top line reads 'Stare'.  I
