Subject: NNET / BRAIN: The Case of the Photocopied Holograms

Steve Whitrow describes some so-called "holograms" which provide 3D
images if you stare at them and relax your eyes.  Of course, these
are not actually holograms, which require resolution of close to the
wavelength of light, far finer than is available by a fax machine.

>From his description, these sound like random-dot stereograms.  Relaxing
your eyes allows them to diverge so that each eye is looking at a
different part of the paper.  The random dots are repeated so that the
images seen by each eye fuse to form a single image.  By offsetting the
random dots in some areas, the amount of divergence needed to fuse the
images varies just as it does when looking at a three-dimensional
object.

My son gets a magazine called "3-2-1-Contact" which used some random-dot
stereograms last year to form a rebus puzzle.

